\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Exterior weights give the finite spin characters}

\p{Work over the complex split models and positive systems fixed in the
preceding type-B and type-D cards. In type #{B_\ell} assume
#{\ell\geq 1}; in type #{D_\ell} assume #{\ell\geq 4}. For
#{I\subseteq\{1,\ldots,\ell\}}, put

##{\lambda_I=\tfrac12\left(
\sum_{i\in I}\epsilon_i-\sum_{i\notin I}\epsilon_i\right).}

By \ref{fcap-0025}, the line #{\mathbb C u_I} has weight #{\lambda_I};
distinct subsets give distinct weights. Consequently

##{\operatorname{ch}S_{B_\ell}
=\sum_I e^{\lambda_I}
=\prod_{i=1}^{\ell}
\left(e^{\epsilon_i/2}+e^{-\epsilon_i/2}\right),}

and the type-D parity carriers have

##{\operatorname{ch}S_D^+
=\sum_{\lvert I\rvert\ {\rm even}}e^{\lambda_I},\qquad
\operatorname{ch}S_D^-
=\sum_{\lvert I\rvert\ {\rm odd}}e^{\lambda_I}.}

If #{a_i=e^{\epsilon_i/2}} and #{b_i=e^{-\epsilon_i/2}}, sorting the
product by occupied parity gives

##{\operatorname{ch}S_D^\pm
=\tfrac12\left[
\prod_i(a_i+b_i)\ \mathbin{\pm}\ \prod_i(b_i-a_i)\right].}

The order #{b_i-a_i} makes an occupied coordinate contribute the minus
sign. These are finite sums obtained from the exterior basis, without
invoking a general character formula.}

\p{The complete half-sum weights above become honest characters on the
Spin torus. Put
#{A_i=e_i e_i'}, so its action is #{N_i}. For
#{t\in\mathbb C^\times}, define

##{g_i(t)=tA_i+t^{-1}(1-A_i)
=(t e_i+t^{-1}e_i')(e_i+e_i').}

Both vector factors have quadratic value #{1}; the pair criterion
therefore puts #{g_i(t)} in the actual Spin carrier
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.ι_mul_ι_mem_spinGroup_of_norm_mul_norm_eq_one}.
The elements for different coordinates commute, and

##{g_i(t)u_I=
\begin{cases}t\,u_I,&i\in I,\\t^{-1}u_I,&i\notin I.\end{cases}}

Their vector action scales #{e_i} by #{t^2}, #{e_i'} by #{t^{-2}}, and
fixes the other coordinates. Thus
#{g(t_1,\ldots,t_\ell)=\prod_i g_i(t_i)} covers the standard orthogonal
torus by coordinatewise squaring.}

\p{The kernel of this parameterization is

##{\{(t_i):t_i\in\{1,-1\},\ \prod_i t_i=1\}.}

Indeed, equality of its spinor action with the identity forces
#{t_i^2=1} by comparing weights that differ in coordinate #{i}, and the
vacuum weight then forces #{\prod_i t_i=1}. Conversely such a tuple gives
the actual Clifford scalar #{\prod_i t_i=1}. Thus both implications hold
directly for the group parameterization. The image contains the scalar
#{-1}; together with the usual
#{\{1,-1\}} kernel of #{\Spin\to SO}
\citet{Proposition 20.28, pp. 308--309}{fulton2004representation},
this is the full inverse-image torus.}

\p{A Laurent monomial on the parameter torus descends through the
displayed sign kernel exactly when all its integer exponents have the
same parity. Relative to the orthogonal coordinates #{x_i=t_i^2}, the
Spin weight lattice is therefore

##{P=\mathbb Z^\ell\cup
\left(\mathbb Z+\tfrac12\right)^\ell.}

The complete signed sums #{\lambda_I} lie in #{P}, while an individual
symbol #{e^{\epsilon_i/2}} need not itself be a character. The orthogonal
torus has character lattice #{\mathbb Z^\ell}; every spinor half-sum
takes the scalar #{-1} to #{-1} and hence does not descend to it. In type
D the adjoint torus has the still smaller root lattice.}

\p{\citet{Corollary 6.3.9, p. 322; Sections 7.1 and 7.2.3,
pp. 330 and 346}{goodman2009symmetry}
independently identify complex Spin as algebraically simply connected,
explain the single-valued square-root torus factors, and identify the
torus-character lattice with the weight lattice. Those group results
support the character interpretation. The pinned TauCeti results used
above are Lie and Clifford statements; no formal integration or
differentiation comparison is asserted.}

\p{For #{B_2}, the four distinct weights are summarized by

##{\operatorname{ch}S_{B_2}
=(x^{1/2}+x^{-1/2})(y^{1/2}+y^{-1/2}),}

with highest weight #{(\epsilon_1+\epsilon_2)/2=\omega_2}. For #{D_4},
the even- and odd-cardinality sums contain eight weights each:
#{\omega_4=(\epsilon_1+\epsilon_2+\epsilon_3+\epsilon_4)/2} lies in
#{S^+}, while
#{\omega_3=(\epsilon_1+\epsilon_2+\epsilon_3-\epsilon_4)/2} lies in
#{S^-}. This is a finite character check, not a triality claim.}
